WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.911
Many practical atomic uses involve flags, counters, and small state machines.

2
00:00:03.911 --> 00:00:11.023
These patterns can be safe and readable when each value has a defined purpose and the surrounding lifecycle is clear.

3
00:00:11.023 --> 00:00:15.646
Stop flags are common, but the flag is only one part of shutdown.

4
00:00:15.646 --> 00:00:24.180
A worker must check the flag at appropriate points, stop accepting or processing work according to policy, release resources safely, and be joined or

5
00:00:24.180 --> 00:00:26.313
otherwise managed before referenced state disappears.

6
00:00:26.313 --> 00:00:28.091
Readiness flags imply publication rules.

7
00:00:28.091 --> 00:00:37.692
If one thread initializes data and then marks it ready, reviewers need to know how the data is published, which ordering is used, and how long the

8
00:00:37.692 --> 00:00:39.470
data remains valid for readers.

9
00:00:39.470 --> 00:00:41.604
Metrics counters may have different requirements.

10
00:00:41.604 --> 00:00:49.071
A counter used only for approximate telemetry may not need the same ordering as a flag used to coordinate object visibility.

11
00:00:49.071 --> 00:00:51.916
That distinction should be intentional rather than accidental.

12
00:00:51.916 --> 00:00:55.472
Atomic state machines should define valid states and allowed transitions.

13
00:00:55.472 --> 00:01:01.161
Prefer named states over magic integers, and keep the transition logic close to the atomic value.

14
00:01:01.161 --> 00:01:05.428
If many unrelated states accumulate, a dedicated synchronized object may be clearer.

15
00:01:05.428 --> 00:01:10.051
Compare-and-exchange can enforce state transitions, but retry behavior must be understandable.

16
00:01:10.051 --> 00:01:18.229
Reviewers should know what happens when another thread changes the value first and whether the operation should retry, return, or report a state

17
00:01:18.229 --> 00:01:18.585
mismatch.

18
00:01:18.585 --> 00:01:20.718
Keep atomic coordination narrow and testable.

19
00:01:20.718 --> 00:01:28.541
The best atomic state is small enough that a reviewer can list the readers, writers, transitions, ordering assumptions, and shutdown behavior without

20
00:01:28.541 --> 00:01:29.964
reconstructing the whole program.

21
00:01:29.964 --> 00:01:36.364
If the list becomes long, split the state, name the protocol, or use a higher-level synchronization mechanism.

22
00:01:36.364 --> 00:01:38.853
Small protocols are easier to test repeatedly.

23
00:01:38.853 --> 00:01:39.920
Name them clearly.
